3.text:
The two curves can meet only for $Q^{3}>2$ and

\begin{equation*}
P_{s}(Q)=\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right) ,\qquad   
P_{d}(Q)=18\,\exp \!\big[-\arctan Q\big] ,
\end{equation*}
so that on the market

\begin{equation*}
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)=18\;e^{-\arctan Q}\equiv P_{s}(Q)=P_{d}(Q)
\end{equation*}
has to be solved.  The function
$f(Q)=\ln(Q^{3}-2)-18e^{-\arctan Q}$ is monotone increasing for
$Q>{\rm cubert\,2}$ and

* $f(4.68)\approx-1.0\times10^{-2}$ ,
* $f(4.70)\approx+5.2\times10^{-3}$ ,

so that with a Newton / bisection iteration

\begin{equation*}
Q^{*}\approx 4.69\!-\!4.70\approx 4.7,
\end{equation*}
and

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}=P_{s}(Q^{*})=P_{d}(Q^{*})
       =\ln\!\big[(Q^{*})^{3}-2\big]
       =18e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7 ,
\end{equation*}
about $P^{*}\simeq 4.62$ in the units of the model.  In particular
$P_{s}(4.693)\simeq4.62$ and $P_{d}(4.693)\simeq4.62$.

Put $W_{d}(Q)=\int_{0}^{Q}P_{d}(q)dq$ and
$W_{s}(Q)=\int_{q_{0}}^{Q}P_{s}(q)dq$ with the zero–price point of the
supply
$\ln(q_{0}^{3}-2)=0\iff q_{0}={\rm cubert\,3}\simeq1.44$.
With $u=\arctan q$

\begin{equation*}
W_{d}(Q)=18\int_{0}^{Q}e^{-\arctan q}dq
       =18\int_{0}^{\arctan Q}e^{-u}\sec^{2}u\,du .
\end{equation*}
Numerically

\begin{equation*}
\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}e^{-\arctan q}dq\approx1.80\!-\!1.81,
\qquad 
W_{d}(Q^{*})\approx18\times1.803\approx 3.25\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}
in the same monetary units.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
CS(Q^{*})=W_{d}(Q^{*})-P^{*}Q^{*}
        \approx 32.5-4.62\times4.69\approx 1.0\!-\!1.1\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}
about $10.8$  in the numerical example above.

For the supply

\begin{equation*}
W_{s}(Q)=\int_{q_{0}}^{Q}\!\ln\!\big(q^{3}-2\big)dq
       =\Big[q\ln(q^{3}-2)-3q\Big]_{q_{0}}^{Q}
         -6\int_{q_{0}}^{Q}\!\frac{dq}{q^{3}-2},
\end{equation*}
and numerically

\begin{equation*}
W_{s}(Q^{*})=\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\ln(q^{3}-2)dq\approx 9.9,
\end{equation*}
so that the producer surplus on the usual $q_{0}$–$Q^{*}$ interval is

\begin{equation*}
PS(Q^{*})=P^{*}Q^{*}-W_{s}(Q^{*})
       \approx 21.68-9.89\approx 11.8 .
\end{equation*}

Hence, on the conventional $CS+PS$ definition

\begin{equation*}
TS(Q^{*})=CS(Q^{*})+PS(Q^{*})
        =W_{d}(Q^{*})-W_{s}(Q^{*})
        =\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\!\big[P_{d}(q)-P_{s}(q)\big]dq
        \approx 22\!-\!23
\end{equation*}
in the model units, about $22.6$ with the $Q^{*}\approx4.69$, 
$P^{*}\approx4.62$ equilibrium quoted above, $CS\approx10.8$ and
$PS\approx11.8$.  In particular
$W_{d}(Q^{*})\approx\$32\!-\!33$ is the total willingness to pay up to
the equilibrium quantity, $P^{*}Q^{*}\approx\$21.7$ is the market value
and the net social surplus between demand and (zero-price) supply is the
$\approx \$22\!-\!23$ quoted above.  If total welfare is quoted as the
un-discounted area under demand, $W_{d}(Q^{*})\approx3.2\!-\!3.3\times10^{1}$,
which with the numbers above is $\,W_{d}(Q^{*})\approx32.5\,$ monetary
units at the competitive equilibrium $Q^{*}\simeq4.7$, $P^{*}\simeq4.6$.

